    CEC bench answer file - Windows 11 Pro, x64.

    GOAL: zero technician clicks from "boot the installer" to "the provisioning
    pipeline is running on screen". The tech picks the target disk (deliberately
    NOT automated - see DiskConfiguration below), and everything after that is
    hands-off through OOBE, auto-logon, and the CEC-Autosetup pipeline starting
    itself.

    USAGE: place this file named exactly  autounattend.xml  at the ROOT of the
    Windows install USB. Put the tool alongside it as  \CEC-Autosetup\  (the
    launcher below finds it on any drive letter).

    ============================ EDIT BEFORE USE ============================
    Every line tagged CHANGEME must be reviewed for your shop. At minimum:
      * bench account name + password (oobeSystem / UserAccounts + AutoLogon)
      * TimeZone
      * ComputerName scheme
    ==========================================================================

    SECURITY NOTE: the bench account password lives in PLAINTEXT (or trivially
    reversible base64) in this file, on a USB stick that walks around the shop.
    Treat it as a throwaway local bench credential, never a password reused
    anywhere else, and consider having the ship-out stage remove/rename the
    account. See unattend/README.md.

    VERIFICATION STATUS: structure and element names below follow the standard
    Windows unattend schema, but Microsoft has changed OOBE/local-account
    behavior across recent Windows 11 builds. Confirm against a scratch VM on
    the exact image you deploy before trusting it on a customer build. Items
    flagged VERIFY are the ones most likely to have shifted.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PASS 1: windowsPE - the installer environment itself.
         Kills the language/keyboard prompt and the EULA click.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="windowsPE">

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <SetupUILanguage>
                <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            </SetupUILanguage>
            <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>   <!-- CHANGEME if not US keyboard -->
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">

            <UserData>
                <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
                <!--
                    ProductKey here is an EDITION SELECTOR, not activation. On a
                    multi-edition ISO, omitting it makes Setup stop and ask which
                    edition to install - a button press. The value below is
                    Microsoft's published generic Pro key: it selects Pro and
                    does NOT activate. Real activation comes from the board's
                    embedded OEM key or your licensing.
                    Remove this block if your ISO is single-edition Pro.
                -->
                <ProductKey>
                    <Key>W269N-WFGWX-YVC9B-4J6C9-T83GX</Key>
                    <WillShowUI>Never</WillShowUI>
                </ProductKey>
            </UserData>

            <!--
                ============ DISK CONFIGURATION: DELIBERATELY OMITTED ============
                An automated <DiskConfiguration> block that wipes "DiskID 0" is
                the single most dangerous thing in an answer file. DiskID
                ordering is NOT guaranteed to match what the tech thinks is the
                boot drive - on a build with multiple NVMe/SATA devices (or a
                customer's data drive plugged in for migration) an automated
                wipe can destroy the wrong disk irreversibly.

                CEC's stance: the tech picks the target disk. That is ONE screen,
                and it is the one screen worth keeping.

                If you decide the bench is controlled enough to automate it,
                uncomment and adapt the block below - and confirm DiskID on the
                exact hardware first (diskpart > list disk in the installer,
                Shift+F10). GPT/UEFI layout shown.

            <DiskConfiguration>
                <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
                <Disk wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <DiskID>0</DiskID>
                    <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
                    <CreatePartitions>
                        <CreatePartition wcm:action="add"><Order>1</Order><Type>EFI</Type><Size>260</Size></CreatePartition>
                        <CreatePartition wcm:action="add"><Order>2</Order><Type>MSR</Type><Size>16</Size></CreatePartition>
                        <CreatePartition wcm:action="add"><Order>3</Order><Type>Primary</Type><Extend>true</Extend></CreatePartition>
                    </CreatePartitions>
                    <ModifyPartitions>
                        <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add"><Order>1</Order><PartitionID>1</PartitionID><Label>System</Label><Format>FAT32</Format></ModifyPartition>
                        <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add"><Order>2</Order><PartitionID>2</PartitionID></ModifyPartition>
                        <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add"><Order>3</Order><PartitionID>3</PartitionID><Label>Windows</Label><Letter>C</Letter><Format>NTFS</Format></ModifyPartition>
                    </ModifyPartitions>
                </Disk>
            </DiskConfiguration>
            <ImageInstall>
                <OSImage>
                    <InstallTo><DiskID>0</DiskID><PartitionID>3</PartitionID></InstallTo>
                </OSImage>
            </ImageInstall>
                ================================================================
            -->
        </component>
    </settings>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PASS 2: specialize - runs after image apply, before OOBE.
         Machine identity + the UAC change, so UAC is already off by the time
         the bench account logs on (EnableLUA takes effect on reboot, and a
         reboot happens between this pass and first logon).
         ===================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="specialize">

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <!-- '*' = random name. CHANGEME to your order-number scheme if you
                 want the name to carry the work order (the pipeline reads the
                 machine name into its reports). -->
            <ComputerName>*</ComputerName>
            <TimeZone>Central Standard Time</TimeZone>   <!-- CHANGEME -->
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <RunSynchronous>
                <!--
                    UAC OFF for the provisioning window. This is the "preferred
                    permanent home" the pipeline's docs point at: with EnableLUA=0
                    set here, the bench admin's token is unsplit at first logon,
                    so the pipeline and its resume task never hit a consent
                    prompt on an unattended reboot.

                    The pipeline's WindowsPrep stage detects this is already 0 and
                    records it, and the ship-out stage restores it before delivery.
                    KEEP THESE IN SYNC: if you remove this, the runtime stage sets
                    it instead (one extra reboot for it to take effect).
                -->
                <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <Order>1</Order>
                    <Description>Disable UAC for the provisioning window</Description>
                    <Path>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System" /v EnableLUA /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f</Path>
                </RunSynchronousCommand>
            </RunSynchronous>
        </component>
    </settings>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PASS 3: oobeSystem - the out-of-box experience.
         This is where the click-elimination happens.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="oobeSystem">

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>   <!-- CHANGEME with the WinPE one -->
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">

            <OOBE>
                <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
                <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
                <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
                <!-- VERIFY: online-account screen suppression is the element
                     Microsoft has churned most across Win11 builds. Defining a
                     LocalAccount below is the load-bearing part; these are the
                     belt-and-braces. -->
                <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
                <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
                <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>   <!-- 3 = skip all express settings -->
                <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
            </OOBE>

            <!--
                A LOCAL account is what actually bypasses the "sign in with a
                Microsoft account" wall. CHANGEME: name + password.
                The password is stored here in the clear - throwaway bench
                credential only. See README security note.
            -->
            <UserAccounts>
                <LocalAccounts>
                    <LocalAccount wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                        <Name>CECBench</Name>                  <!-- CHANGEME -->
                        <DisplayName>CEC Bench</DisplayName>
                        <Group>Administrators</Group>
                        <Password>
                            <Value>ChangeMe-BenchOnly</Value>  <!-- CHANGEME -->
                            <PlainText>true</PlainText>
                        </Password>
                    </LocalAccount>
                </LocalAccounts>
            </UserAccounts>

            <!--
                Auto-logon so the pipeline starts with no keyboard touch.
                LogonCount governs how many boots auto-login before Windows
                reverts to the normal logon screen.

                IMPORTANT: the pipeline REBOOTS several times (BIOS hand-off,
                Windows Update cycles, driver-order restart boundaries). Each
                consumes one auto-logon. 12 is deliberately generous so a
                multi-reboot WU catch-up can't strand the run at a logon prompt.
                The pipeline's resume task runs as SYSTEM and does not depend on
                this, but the tech-visible console does.
            -->
            <AutoLogon>
                <Enabled>true</Enabled>
                <LogonCount>12</LogonCount>
                <Username>CECBench</Username>                  <!-- CHANGEME: match above -->
                <Password>
                    <Value>ChangeMe-BenchOnly</Value>          <!-- CHANGEME: match above -->
                    <PlainText>true</PlainText>
                </Password>
            </AutoLogon>

            <!--
                THE HAND-OFF: find CEC-Autosetup on whatever drive letter the
                stick landed on and start the pipeline in its own visible window.

                Why this shape:
                  * Drive-letter agnostic - scans every filesystem root for
                    \CEC-Autosetup\bootstrap.ps1, then a flat \bootstrap.ps1.
                  * Start-Process + return: FirstLogonCommands finishes
                    immediately so the desktop appears, while the pipeline runs
                    in its own -NoExit window the tech can watch and scroll.
                  * If the stick is missing, it says so loudly instead of
                    failing silently.

                DRY-RUN VARIANT: drop the '-Install' argument to make a bare run,
                which performs the readiness SELF-CHECK and installs nothing.
                Recommended for the first machine you test this file on.
            -->
            <FirstLogonCommands>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <Order>1</Order>
                    <Description>Launch CEC-Autosetup provisioning pipeline</Description>
                    <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
                    <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "$c='CEC-Autosetup\bootstrap.ps1','bootstrap.ps1'; $b=$null; foreach ($d in (Get-PSDrive -PSProvider FileSystem).Root) { foreach ($x in $c) { $p=Join-Path $d $x; if ((Test-Path $p) -and (-not $b)) { $b=$p } } }; if ($b) { Start-Process powershell.exe -ArgumentList @('-NoProfile','-ExecutionPolicy','Bypass','-NoExit','-File',$b,'-Install') } else { Start-Process powershell.exe -ArgumentList @('-NoProfile','-NoExit','-Command','Write-Host ''CEC-Autosetup not found on any drive. Insert the stick and run bootstrap.ps1 -Install manually.'' -ForegroundColor Red') }"</CommandLine>
                </SynchronousCommand>
            </FirstLogonCommands>
        </component>
    </settings>
</unattend>
